            "authors": "Jianliang Huang, Xuejun Feng*, Suyang Wang, Cheng Zhang",
            "keywords": "Long-distance restricted waterway; maritime scheduling rules; simulation; channel navigation efficiency; berth utilization rate.",
            "abstract": "According to mixed ship flow characteristics in specific time periods, optimizing maritime scheduling rules is a low-cost and efficient method to improve channel navigation efficiency. In this paper, the improvement of long-distance restricted channel navigation efficiency was taken as the research object, and the average waiting time (AWT), port service level (AWT/AST), berth utilization rate (ρ), and channel passage capacity (T) were taken as the indicators. Based on the AIS data of docking ships, the Arena port production operation simulation system was established to analyze the impact of maritime scheduling rules, such as first come, first served (FCFS), port efficiency priority (PEP), and comprehensive benefit priority (CBP), on navigation efficiency. Using the RSOT long-distance restricted channel as a case, the research findings based on AIS data from docking ships in 2021–2022 show that the impacts of FCFS and PEP rules are essentially the same. CBP rules can effectively reduce the average waiting time of most ship types while improving the port service level. Among them, the AWT of 100,000-ton crude oil ships is decreased by 62% at most, and the berth utilization rate of cargo ships is increased by 64%. When the current vessel arrival density flow is increased by 20%, high efficiency is still maintained, and the AWT and AWT/AST of refined oil ships are decreased by 62% at most. The berth utilization rates of cargo ship berths, crude oil berths, and oil product berths are increased by 67%, 28%, and 26%, respectively, and the T value can be increased by 14%. The research conclusions can provide a reference for optimizing maritime scheduling rules and improving navigation efficiency for this kind of long-distance restricted channel.",
